===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|Shia}}, {{l|en|Shiah}}, {{l|en|Shi'ah}}

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|ar|شِيعَة|t=followers, partisans}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|uncountable}} The [[branch]] of [[Islam]] that [[believe]]s that [[Ali]] [[succeed]]ed [[Muhammad]] as [[leader]]{{,}} and that places emphasis on the prophet's family.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1998|author={{w|Geert H. Hofstede}}|title=Masculinity and Femininity: The Taboo Dimension of National Cultures| page=205| url=http://books.google.com/books?id=biIQaxmGULgC&pg=PA205&dq=%22Shia%22#v=onepage| passage=In Islam, Sunni is a more triumphant version of the faith than '''Shia''', which stresses the importance of suffering, following the founder Ali, who was persecuted.}}
#* '''2008''', J. Gordon Melton, ''The Encyclopedia of Religious Phenomena'', Visible Ink Press, [http://books.google.com/books?id=SOldV3dVOoUC&pg=PA181&dq=%22Shi%27a%22#v=onepage page 181]:
#*: '''Shi’a''' is one of the two larger divisions of Islam, {{...}}
#* ''a''. '''2013''', Bruce Anthony Collet, ''Refugee Education as a Gauge of Liberal Multiculturalism: Iraqi Students in Jordan and the United States'' in 2013, Heidi Biseth, Halla B. Holmarsdottir, ''Human Rights in the Field of Comparative Education'', Sense Publishers, [http://books.google.com/books?id=1RdLAAAAQBAJ&pg=PA158&dq=%22Shi%27a%22#v=onepage page 158]:
#*: Jordanians do not know what '''Shi’a''' is about, and it was very recently, [the] last few years, when we start[ed] being exposed to '''Shi’a'''.
# {{lb|en|countable}} One who follows Shi'a Islam; a [[Shiite]].

